WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.834
-Welcome back to the show.
-Oh, my gosh, in person.

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:04.133
-And it's great
to see you in person.

00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:06.334
-Yes, in the flesh.
-Thank you so much for --

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:08.968
You helped us out on Zoom.
And then, we actually did --

00:00:08.968 --> 00:00:10.234
-Well, I didn't help you.
-You did, you --

00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:12.200
-I just was there once.
-No -- well, that --

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.133
that's helping our show,
so I appreciate that.

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:15.834
Thank you so much
for doing that.

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:19.067
We also did a great bit together
based off of "WandaVision,"

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:20.267
and that was one of
our best bits

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:21.501
that we've ever done
on the show.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.100
Congrats on "WandaVision,"
by the way.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.334
Since I talked to you --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:27.234
It was so great.

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:29.434
-It's so funny.
-It was fantastic.

00:00:29.434 --> 00:00:32.133
It got nominated for
like 20 Emmy nominations.

00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:34.300
And I was like, "Oh, my gosh."
And, of course, it was the first

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.200
kind of Emmys back where
everyone just kind of came back.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.267
What was that like for you,
that experience of having

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:41.501
an award show
and everyone around?

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:44.400
-It was wei--
I mean, it was very surprising.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.067
No one --
No one does a Marvel job

00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:48.534
and thinks that you're going to
get nominated for something.

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:50.901
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah -- but you did.

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:52.133
You crushed it.
-But we did.

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.501
And then we actually
got to celebrate together

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:57.901
for the first time at the Emmys
because we wrapped during COVID.

00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:00.534
And I wish -- I wish
that everyone was together,

00:01:00.534 --> 00:01:02.167
like, department heads
and everything.

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:04.868
But it was really special
getting to be there

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:08.334
and celebrate our show among,
like, you know,

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.701
like, really serious things.
-Yeah.

00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:11.901
-We just had a good time.
-Well, it was so good

00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:14.400
and different and creative
that you had to be

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.367
applauded for that.
It was just so fun to watch.

00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:19.334
And obviously, we'll talk about
"Doctor Strange."

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:21.067
But congrats on that.
You nailed it.

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:22.334
"WandaVision" was so good.
-Thanks.

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:23.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thanks.

00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:25.801
-I was happy for you.
But you have --

00:01:25.801 --> 00:01:27.667
you have a new project
coming out

00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:30.701
that I'm excited for
because I have kids.

00:01:30.701 --> 00:01:32.501
You're writing
a children's book.

00:01:32.501 --> 00:01:34.067
You wrote one.
-Yes, my husband and I

00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:35.901
wrote a children's book,
which I didn't know

00:01:35.901 --> 00:01:38.100
I was gonna talk about tonight,
but how great.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.133
-Yeah, you can talk about it,
yeah.

00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:40.968
I figure, why not?
-Why not?

00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:43.734
It comes out the end of June,
and --

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:45.067
-"Hattie Harmony."
-"Hattie Harmony."

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:47.300
She's a worry detective.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.133
And this was a project
that we came up with

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:50.868
during COVID and lockdown.

00:01:50.868 --> 00:01:52.367
-A lot of ideas
came out of that.

00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:55.000
-Yeah, and trying to figure out
what positive stuff to put out

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.033
in the world and how to affect
kids in a positive way.

00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:02.133
And I love talking about
my feelings in therapy.

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.868
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.868 --> 00:02:06.434
-So, yeah --
-So, she's a worry detective,

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:10.234
and she helps her friends at
school identify their feelings.

00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:13.300
And then she has a toolkit,
and she gives them tools

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.267
to help deal with their worries
and their anxieties.

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:18.133
And it's a book series
I'm really excited about.

00:02:18.133 --> 00:02:19.667
-Oh, so you have a second one?

00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:20.667
-We will, yeah.
-Oh, really?

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:22.000
-Next year.
-Congratulations!

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.067
I love -- Hattie Harmony
is a great --

00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:24.200
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.834
-That's a cute name.
-Isn't she cute? I know.

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:27.534
That's my husband.
He's very clever.

00:02:27.534 --> 00:02:30.701
-And Hattie and catty.
I love it, yeah.

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:32.634
You know, I was reading up
on you before this interview

00:02:32.634 --> 00:02:34.167
just to see
if there's anything else

00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:36.634
that we haven't talked about,
and I saw that you got

00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:39.200
your real estate license
before your career took off.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.734
-I did.
-In New York City?

00:02:40.734 --> 00:02:42.634
-While I was in college.
-Really?

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:46.067
-Yeah, I just wanted a summer
job that wasn't at a restaurant?

00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:47.167
-You couldn't -- why?

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:48.167
You couldn't work
at a restaurant?

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:50.400
-I just -- you know, I --

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.400
I think I'm really patient
with people,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.734
but I don't know
if I actually would've been able

00:02:54.734 --> 00:02:56.601
to deal with that every day.

00:02:56.601 --> 00:02:58.667
I feel like
"the customer's always right"

00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:00.701
isn't the most fair thing
in the world.

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:03.067
-Yeah, there's some customers
that aren't right.

00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:04.801
-They're really not right.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.801 --> 00:03:06.934
-Yeah, no, they're not right.
Yeah, treat people with respect.

00:03:06.934 --> 00:03:09.901
Yeah, calm down.
-So, I felt real estate --

00:03:09.901 --> 00:03:12.667
I liked -- whatever.
I just, like -- it was easy.

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:14.801
I mean, I just --
Getting the license wasn't easy.

00:03:14.801 --> 00:03:16.267
That was actually annoying.
But --

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:18.067
-What do you have to do
for a real-estate license?

00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:20.267
-You just take classes.

00:03:20.267 --> 00:03:24.234
And I didn't, like, sell things.
I worked for someone who did.

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:25.868
-Did you go to apartment
showings?

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:27.133
-Yes.
-You go like,

00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:28.367
"Why don't you come take a look
at the kitchen?

00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:30.100
It's really spacious."
-And I, like, went in

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.534
and, like, turned lights on.

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:33.567
That's, like,
a really big part of my job.

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:34.767
-Wow, nailed it.
Wow.

00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:37.000
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.300
-Wow.
Isn't that unbelievable?

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.100
-But I would, like,
be booking these appointments,

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.501
and I just moved to New York.
I was 19 years old.

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:44.267
And I'd be booking
people's appointments,

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:46.701
and I'd come up with street
names that I didn't --

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:50.100
I didn't know how to pronounce.
And there is a street

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.701
that is very familiar
to me now...

00:03:51.701 --> 00:03:54.868
-Like Houston? Or --
-Well, that one I mess up now

00:03:54.868 --> 00:03:56.100
after living in New York
for eight years.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.367
-You're like, "Oh,
it's on 'Hue-ston' Street."

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.667
-No, it was Stuyvesant.
-Stuyvesant.

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:01.234
-Yeah, so, I was making
an appointment.

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:02.901
I was like,
"Our client wants to see, like,

00:04:02.901 --> 00:04:06.000
46 'Stew-vee-san' Street."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.133
-Wow!
Stuyvesant you made --

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:09.968
-"Stew-vee-san."
-"Stew-vee-san"?

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:11.133
-Yeah.
-Stuy-town?

00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:12.267
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:13.601
-I didn't --
wasn't familiar with it yet.

00:04:13.601 --> 00:04:15.400
-That's so perfect.
That's fantastic.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.267
-But no one told me
when I was doing it.

00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:18.801
They all were just silent
while I was on the phone.

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:20.400
-"Stew-vee-san"?
-And they didn't correct me.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.868
And then they all just laughed.

00:04:21.868 --> 00:04:23.300
-You could jack the prices up
if you lived in --

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.868
[ Laughter ]
-I know. In Stuy-town.

00:04:24.868 --> 00:04:26.234
"Stew-vee-san" Town.
-"Stew-vee-san."

00:04:26.234 --> 00:04:28.167
"Stew-vee-san" Town.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:30.601
It sounds so French.
It's so cool.

00:04:30.601 --> 00:04:31.868
-Yeah.
-We went --

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:34.200
So they didn't like that at all?
It didn't take off?

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.400
-No, it's just a voicemail, and
I was just made fun of after.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.300
-"Stew-vee-san" -- I love that!

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.234
I'm always gonna
call it that from now on.

00:04:40.234 --> 00:04:41.934
I remember learning
what SoHo was.

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:43.934
I was like,
"Oh, SoHo, that's so cool."

00:04:43.934 --> 00:04:45.534
It just means South of Houston.
-South of Houston.

00:04:45.534 --> 00:04:47.400
Yeah, everything.
Dumbo, every -- it's all --

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.667
-Yeah, I didn't know what
any of that stuff meant.

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:50.100
-Which is cool.
-Yes.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.801
-There is a coolness.
-We made it cool.

00:04:51.801 --> 00:04:53.501
-Yeah.
-Yeah. NoMad.

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:54.734
-Yeah.
-North of Madison.

00:04:54.734 --> 00:04:56.033
-Sure.
-Yeah.

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:57.300
-I think I just learned that.
-Oh, is that right?

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.734
-Sure.
-All right.

00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:00.267
"Stew-vee-san."
-"Stew-vee-san," NoMad.

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:01.868
-[ French accent ]
We're learning from each other.

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:03.033
Let's talk about
"Doctor Strange."

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.467
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:05.567
-[ Normal voice ]
"Doctor Strange

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:07.033
in the Multiverse of Madness."

00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:09.267
Oh, my gosh.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:11.934
Big, big, big movie
this weekend.

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:14.267
Go see it IMAX if you can.

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:16.067
I know you just had
the big premiere in L.A.

00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:17.567
-Yes.
-How did that go?

00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:19.167
-It was really amazing.
It was --

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:23.000
It's amazing to be in front of
people clapping and screaming.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.067
It's --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:26.033
[ Laughs ]
-You miss that, right?

00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:28.567
-I'm really shallow.
I really need it.

00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:30.701
-So do I.
Please, I need it more than you.

00:05:30.701 --> 00:05:32.734
-But it was --
it was amazing to have

00:05:32.734 --> 00:05:34.000
a premiere in front of fans.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.434
I don't watch the --
I didn't watch it.

00:05:36.434 --> 00:05:37.868
I haven't seen it.
-You don't watch yourself.

00:05:37.868 --> 00:05:39.934
-No, I do watch my stuff.
I just decided

00:05:39.934 --> 00:05:42.667
I can't watch these Marvel
movies at a premiere anymore.

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:45.200
-Why?
-Well, every time,

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.467
I just watch it,
and I look around me,

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:48.868
and I'm like,
"Well, it's our first flop,"

00:05:48.868 --> 00:05:50.133
like, every time.

00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:52.167
I just think,
"It's our first flop."

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:55.934
-You think you --
-I saw "Avengers: Endgame,"

00:05:55.934 --> 00:05:58.534
and I looked around me, and I
said, "Is this our first flop?"

00:05:58.534 --> 00:06:00.701
-"Avengers: Endgame"
is one of the biggest movies

00:06:00.701 --> 00:06:02.067
in the history of the world.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:02.067 --> 00:06:03.601
-And you were like, "Here we go.
We got our first flop."

00:06:03.601 --> 00:06:05.334
-I was like, "I don't know.
I don't know about this one."

00:06:05.334 --> 00:06:06.868
-"No one's gonna watch
this 'Avengers' thing."

00:06:06.868 --> 00:06:09.467
-Yeah. And so, I just decided
I don't want to put myself

00:06:09.467 --> 00:06:11.534
through that experience again.
-Oh, it is so --

00:06:11.534 --> 00:06:14.267
-So I'll see it at some point.
-It is so not a flop.

00:06:14.267 --> 00:06:17.400
And you are getting
amazing reviews, by the way.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:18.868
You just pop.
You're stunning in this thing.

00:06:18.868 --> 00:06:20.667
-I'm a popper.
-You do. You pop.

00:06:20.667 --> 00:06:22.167
You come out.
I mean --

00:06:22.167 --> 00:06:23.234
Do you read the reviews
or you go,

00:06:23.234 --> 00:06:25.367
"I can't get that involved,"
either?

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:27.267
-I see them
when they're sent to me.

00:06:27.267 --> 00:06:30.234
Michael Waldron, who wrote it,
sent me a clip of --

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:33.834
Just a segment of one,
and it was such an absurd quote,

00:06:33.834 --> 00:06:36.901
that I just told him that, like,
"You're mean, and you're lying,"

00:06:36.901 --> 00:06:38.701
and then he sent me the article.

00:06:38.701 --> 00:06:41.634
And I went to the article,
and the guy's name was Mick.

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:44.734
And I was like,
"Michael, what a bad alias."

00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:47.567
-Oh. You though Michael --
he was Mick.

00:06:47.567 --> 00:06:49.667
-"Yeah, okay, Michael."
-Wonder who this "Mick" guy is.

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:51.667
-Where do you live?
In "Stew-vee-san?"

00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:52.934
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, please.

00:06:52.934 --> 00:06:54.367
-"I can see you."
-"I can see you."

00:06:54.367 --> 00:06:58.534
"Yeah, I know names, buddy."
No, I say Mick is a cool name.

00:06:58.534 --> 00:07:00.968
There --
There's a theory online --

00:07:00.968 --> 00:07:02.667
Of course, no spoilers.

00:07:02.667 --> 00:07:03.767
I don't want to tell anyone
anything.

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:04.834
-No spoilers.
-Never.

00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:07.400
But there's some goodies
in this one.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.534
Yeah, there's some cameos,
maybe?

00:07:09.534 --> 00:07:11.300
-Maybe.
I was hardly there.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.601
-You can't say anything, yeah.

00:07:12.601 --> 00:07:14.234
-It was just me and a
greenscreen and no other actors.

00:07:14.234 --> 00:07:16.067
-Just plug your ears a little
bit and close your eyes.

00:07:16.067 --> 00:07:17.934
-Sure.
But do you want to tell them?

00:07:17.934 --> 00:07:19.868
-No, I'm just gonna say
that there are cameos.

00:07:19.868 --> 00:07:21.400
-Oh, yes.
Sure, sure, sure, sure, sure.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:23.868
-I wouldn't say who it is.
But then, there's theories

00:07:23.868 --> 00:07:27.133
online that say maybe
your character in this film

00:07:27.133 --> 00:07:29.734
ends up being the villain.

00:07:29.734 --> 00:07:32.234
-Yes, I've heard about this.
-I've seen the film.

00:07:32.234 --> 00:07:34.534
I can't -- this is me acting.
This is my poker face.

00:07:34.534 --> 00:07:38.133
-Well, you know,
I don't think of any character

00:07:38.133 --> 00:07:41.834
I play as a villain
or a bad guy or a good guy.

00:07:41.834 --> 00:07:43.701
-Of course not.
We love Elizabeth Olsen.

00:07:43.701 --> 00:07:46.434
-No, we just --
I love my characters.

00:07:46.434 --> 00:07:47.968
-Oh, okay.
-You know, I want to --

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:50.501
I'm their advocate.
I defend them.

00:07:50.501 --> 00:07:52.434
-Okay.
-I just played an axe murderer.

00:07:52.434 --> 00:07:53.634
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.634 --> 00:07:56.100
And I love her,
and I think she's fabulous.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.033
-Wow, wow!
All right, so --

00:07:58.033 --> 00:07:59.767
-Coming to you in fall.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:59.767 --> 00:08:01.667
-Bring the whole family!

00:08:01.667 --> 00:08:05.133
Not in this one.
Well, congrats on everything.

00:08:05.133 --> 00:08:07.133
I want to show everyone a clip.
Here's Elizabeth Olsen

00:08:07.133 --> 00:08:08.501
and Benedict Cumberbatch in

00:08:08.501 --> 00:08:10.801
"Doctor Strange
in the Multiverse of Madness."

00:08:10.801 --> 00:08:12.334
Take a look at this.

00:08:12.334 --> 00:08:14.167
-What do you know about
the multiverse?

00:08:14.167 --> 00:08:16.067
-The multiverse.

00:08:16.067 --> 00:08:17.467
Vis had his theories.

00:08:17.467 --> 00:08:21.334
He believed it was real
and...dangerous.

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:23.634
-Well, he was right about both.

00:08:23.634 --> 00:08:26.434
We found a girl who can somehow
travel across it,

00:08:26.434 --> 00:08:28.033
but she's being pursued.

00:08:28.033 --> 00:08:29.767
-Pursued by who?
-Some kind of demon,

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:32.434
one that covets her power
for itself.

00:08:32.434 --> 00:08:35.501
We've taken her to Kamar-Taj,
and we've got our defenses,

00:08:35.501 --> 00:08:38.133
but we could use an Avenger.

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:40.601
-There are other Avengers.

00:08:40.601 --> 00:08:42.067
-Yeah, but given the choice
between the archer

00:08:42.067 --> 00:08:45.033
